{
    "background_story": "Lachlan, a young anthropologist, had always been fascinated by the Cushitic languages and cultures of East Africa. He spent years studying the intricacies of Somali, Afar, and Oromo dialects, eventually becoming fluent in all three. His passion led him to Ethiopia, where he immersed himself in the local community, learning about the customs and traditions of the Cushitic-speaking peoples. Lachlan's research focused on the ancient trade routes and cultural exchanges between the Cushitic kingdoms and the Mediterranean world. His groundbreaking work shed new light on the region's rich history, earning him recognition among his peers. As he delved deeper into the Cushitic world, Lachlan discovered a sense of belonging among the people, who welcomed him as one of their own. He became a bridge between cultures, using his knowledge to promote understanding and cooperation between East Africa and the Western world.",
